my first thought when I saw the new 2019  iPad air show up in Apple's website was  wow that's basically a 2017 iPad pro but  with fewer features so I recommended  that instead of the new air but since  then I've realized that not everyone  wants I used a refurbished two year old  iPad and although the a 10x chip in the  iPad pro will last many years the a12  and the new iPad air will last longer in  its fresh and brand-new I've realized  that the $500 price tag really isn't  that bad for what you get considering  that the extra power of the 2018 iPad  pro won't be noticed unless you're doing  something like video editing we played  games on both iPads and didn't really  notice a difference in available  graphics quality and smoothness of  gameplay both of them could easily play  certain games at 60 frames per second  and even pub G at maxed out graphics no  issues at all on the iPad air the 2019  iPad Mini packs the same 812 processor  as the iPad air but for $100 cheaper  which is a great deal if you're okay  with the smaller display and in my  review of the iPad Mini I called it the  best tablet ever for gaming even more so  than the new iPad pro because of the  lightweight and easy to hold form factor  the mini and air are basically identical  iPads except for two things  the size and the fact that the air gets  a smart connector for use with Apple's  high quality smart keyboard the Mini is  so small that typing on a Bluetooth  keyboard won't really be comfortable  other than that they're basically the  same exact iPad the displays are  absolutely fantastic and much better  than the 2018 budget iPads display  however the smaller display of the Mini  is definitely a drawback for certain  things so that's why it's worth it for  some people to spend the extra hundred  dollars for the iPad air if you're into  note-taking or drawing the minis display  is pretty limiting in that regard I  don't think people realize just how much  smaller the display is compared to the  iPad air the video content is much  smaller doing schoolwork or spreadsheets  may be a bit more difficult and you see  less on the screen while browsing the  web so the 2019 iPad air is a great  choice for those who need a larger  display but don't want to pay the extra  cash for the new iPad pro the biggest  problem is that iOS is currently very  limiting so you can't really use you  iPad as a computer replacement at least  not yet now let's take a look into the  future and see what we can expect  iOS 13 is going to be revealed soon and  it looks like there are some iPad  features coming that are going to boost  productivity and multitasking on the  iPad of course there's the rumored dark  mode feature but I want to focus on  productivity features for example it  looks like we're finally getting  multiple app window support so that  means you can have two instances of  different apps open at the same time  like Google Docs - Safari windows or -  spreadsheets and much more and you can  easily move them around the screen just  like you can do on a real computer also  Safari will be able to automatically ask  for a desktop version of certain sites  to avoid compatibility issues and we're  finally getting a real undo gesture  because who's going to be shaking their  iPad to undo text that's ridiculous  Apple's also rumored to be adding a new  font manager and they're making  improvements to document collaboration  and third-party apps the mail app is  getting some worthy desktop like  improvements as well and it seems like  Apple is finally going to add a way to  quickly select multiple items in various  views just like click and drag on Mac OS  as you can tell from these changes Apple  is really focusing on improving workflow  and allowing people to actually replace  their laptops much more easily now if  you're using these features and working  with multiple windows being limited to  the iPad minis display isn't going to be  fun that's why I think the air is going  to be the better choice for people who  use it for studying typing business work  or multitasking and we can look even  further at io s 14 and what features  that will be adding and I'm sure the  extra screen real estate will serve you  better in the years to come now if you  don't do much of that like let's say you  do a lot of gaming and you like the  portability of the iPad Mini then that's  still a great choice for some people  especially since you save $100 and  talking about the new iPad pro I still  think it's overkill for almost everyone  even after I was 13 and 14 are released  the 812 and the iPad air is a very  powerful chip and it will be more than  good enough for most people for years to  come now what are some of the drawbacks  of the 2019 iPad air well the main thing  is that still using the same old home  button packing design to me it's really  not that big of a deal the design still  looks  and works great another downside is that  it's still only packing two speakers on  the bottom  unlike four speakers on the iPad pro it  sucks but it makes sense why Apple would  keep that exclusively for the iPad pro  just like the pro motion technology  other than that there's really nothing  to complain about the iPad air it's a  fantastic all-around iPad that offers  basically everything you'll need to do  iPad things with an incredibly bright  and color accurate display so here's my  recommendation if you don't care much  for the iPad pros extra features and you  want to do some drawings note-taking  typing or multitasking I would  definitely buy the new iPad air over  every other iPad in Apple's 2019 iPad  lineup now if your budget is limited you  can pick up the 2018 budget iPad for  only around 250 to $280 brand-new but  you have to compromise on things like  display quality in a bit of performance  if you enjoyed this review  hit that button above to subscribe and  check out some of those in-depth iPad  comparison videos thanks for watching  and we'll see you in the next video  [Music]
 

